{
  "teamId": 158,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Milwaukee Brewers arrived with something to prove, and Christian Yelich announced intentions immediately — a three-run home run on Opening Day set the tone for what would become a season of genuine contention. March was crisp and promising, but April brought turbulence: a .480 winning percentage and a bullpen that couldn't hold leads, with Trevor Megill and Abner Uribe absorbing damage that cost the Brewers winnable games. The roster was still finding its identity, leaning on Gary Sánchez's timely power and Brice Turang's clutch hitting to stay afloat.\n\nMay changed everything. The Brewers went 19-7, their best month of the season, riding a wave of offensive eruptions and timely pitching. Brice Turang's sixth home run of the year, Jackson Chourio's emerging presence in the lineup, and a defense that started making the right plays at the right moments transformed Milwaukee from a .500 team into a genuine division threat. The swagger of that month carried into June and July, where the Brewers continued to grind out wins at a pace that put them on track for one of the best records in the National League. Aaron Ashby's rough outings — surrendering a grand slam to Esmerlyn Valdez and getting torched by Ozzie Albies — were the exception rather than the rule, blips in an otherwise relentless march forward.\n\nAugust and September saw the Brewers consolidate their excellence. William Contreras's three-run home run on July 19th — his tenth of the year, a ball crushed to center field with Chourio and Turang aboard — captured what this lineup had become: deep, dangerous, and capable of breaking games open at any moment. Jackson Chourio's 22nd home run in September, a two-run shot that plated Christian Yelich, punctuated a 103-win season that left no doubt Milwaukee belonged among the NL's elite. The pitching staff finished with a 3.51 ERA, and the run differential of 214 runs told a complete story.\n\nNow the Brewers are in October, and the stage has only gotten bigger. They dispatched their Division Series opponents with authority — Chourio's line-drive single in Game 2 that scored two and effectively slammed the door remains the defining image of that round. With a 2-0 lead in the NLCS, Milwaukee stands two wins away from the World Series. William Contreras is hitting with power, Garrett Mitchell is covering center field like a force of nature, and the bullpen — Trevor Megill included — is finding its footing when the lights are brightest. The Brewers' October story is still being written, but through two NLCS games, they are writing it in their favor.",
